>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> the best free study planner is one that keeps your <strong>class timetable, homework, exam dates and study sessions in one place</strong>, works on the devices you use every day, and costs nothing for the features you use weekly. For most students that is a free planner app that syncs between phone and laptop, such as <a href="#classtrack">ClassTrack</a> (Android and web) or <a href="#mystudylife">MyStudyLife</a> (with a native iPhone app). If you prefer paper, a free <a href="#printable">printable weekly planner</a> works too.</p>
  <p>This guide compares every kind of free study planner on what the free plan really includes, where the limits are, and who each one suits. It ends with a 30-minute set-up you can follow in any of them.</p>
  <p class="small muted"><strong>Disclosure:</strong> studyplanner.tech is run by the makers of ClassTrack, which is on this list. We list our own limits, we say when another planner is the better choice, and we do not accept paid placements. See <a href="#method">how we researched this guide</a> and our <a href="/about/#editorial">editorial policy</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="quick">Quick picks: the best free study planners in 2026</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">#</th><th scope="col">Free study planner</th><th scope="col">Best for</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {picks.map((p, i) => <tr><td>{i + 1}</td><td><a href={`#${p.id}`}><strong>{p.name}</strong></a></td><td>{p.best}</td></tr>)}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="what">What a free study planner should include</h2>
  <p>A study planner is more than a to-do list. It has to understand that classes repeat every week, that homework belongs to a subject, and that an exam is a deadline you prepare for over many days. Before you choose, check that the free plan covers these six things:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>A weekly class timetable</strong> that repeats, with rooms and times.</li>
    <li><strong>Homework and assignments</strong> with due dates, linked to subjects.</li>
    <li><strong>Exam dates</strong>, ideally with a countdown, so revision starts on time.</li>
    <li><strong>Reminders</strong> that reach you on your phone.</li>
    <li><strong>Sync across devices</strong>, if you use a phone and a laptop.</li>
    <li><strong>A way to spread revision over days</strong>, such as study blocks or a study plan.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Grades, notes, focus timers and AI are useful extras. They should not be the reason you pick a planner you will not open every day.</p>

  <h2 id="types">The 5 kinds of free study planner</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Kind</th><th scope="col">Examples</th><th scope="col">Best for</th><th scope="col">Trade-off</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Student planner app</strong></td><td>ClassTrack, MyStudyLife, Power Planner</td><td>Most students: timetable, homework and exams in one place, with reminders</td><td>Free plans have limits on extras</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Online (web) study planner</strong></td><td>ClassTrack web app, MyStudyLife web</td><td>Planning on a laptop, Chromebook or school computer</td><td>Browsers usually cannot send reminders</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Calendar app</strong></td><td>Google Calendar</td><td>Classes and study blocks as events</td><td>No homework list, grades or exam countdowns</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Build-your-own workspace</strong></td><td>Notion, a spreadsheet</td><td>Students who enjoy designing a system</td><td>Set-up takes hours; nothing is ready-made</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Printable planner</strong></td><td>Our <a href="/templates/">free templates</a></td><td>Students who focus better without a screen</td><td>No reminders; easy to lose</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

