  <p class="small muted">Competitor details come from each product’s official site, store listing or help pages, checked in October 2026. Free plans change, so check the listing before you commit a whole semester.</p>

  <h2 id="picks">The best free study planners, one by one</h2>

  <h3 id="classtrack">1. ClassTrack: best free all-in-one study planner</h3>
  <p><strong>Free on:</strong> Android (<a href={SITE.playUrl} rel="noopener">Google Play</a>) and the web (<a href={SITE.webUrl} rel="noopener">{SITE.webHost}</a>), same account · <strong>Disclosure:</strong> we make it.</p>
  <p>ClassTrack puts your class timetable, homework, exam countdowns, grades and attendance in one free planner. You can import your timetable with AI from a photo, a PDF or pasted text, and the AI can split a subject into topics and spread them up to your exam date. The web app has a desktop layout with a week calendar, so you can plan on a laptop and get reminders from the Android app.</p>
  <p><strong>Free limits:</strong> {f.aiMessages} AI messages a month, {f.timetableScans} AI timetable scans and {f.learningPlans} AI learning plans. The timetable, tasks, exams, grades and reminders have no limits. <strong>Not free:</strong> notes and flashcards, daily Google Calendar import and unlimited AI are in Pro (<a href="/pricing/">pricing</a>).</p>
  <p><strong>Weak spots:</strong> no native iPhone app yet (iPhone users can use the web app in Safari, without reminders) and no rotating Week A/B timetables.</p>

  <h3 id="mystudylife">2. MyStudyLife: best free planner with a native iPhone app</h3>
  <p><strong>Free on:</strong> iPhone, iPad, Android and the web.</p>
  <p>One of the longest-running student planners. It handles weekly, rotating and block timetables, homework with subtasks, exams, reminders and grades, and works offline. It is the safer free choice if you want a native iPhone app or your school uses a rotating timetable. <strong>Weak spots:</strong> some features need a paid upgrade, and we found no attendance tracking in its feature list. Full comparison: <a href="/compare/classtrack-vs-mystudylife/">ClassTrack vs MyStudyLife</a>.</p>

  <h3 id="power-planner">3. Power Planner: best free planner for grades</h3>
  <p><strong>Free on:</strong> iPhone, iPad, Android, Chromebook, Windows, Mac and the web (beta).</p>
  <p>Strong grade tracking: weighted grades, credits and custom GPA scales. The free version is ad-free. <strong>Free limits:</strong> one semester, five grades per class, one repeating bulk entry and one photo per item. Premium is a one-time $4.99 purchase, not a subscription. Full comparison: <a href="/compare/classtrack-vs-power-planner/">ClassTrack vs Power Planner</a>.</p>

  <h3 id="google-calendar">4. Google Calendar: best free calendar for classes</h3>
  <p><strong>Free on:</strong> web, iPhone and Android.</p>
  <p>Add each class as a repeating event and block study time around it. If your school uses Google Classroom, classwork due dates appear in your calendar automatically. <strong>Weak spots:</strong> it is a calendar, so there is no homework checklist, grade tracking or exam countdown.</p>

  <h3 id="notion">5. Notion: best free build-your-own study system</h3>
  <p><strong>Free on:</strong> web, Windows, Mac, iPhone and Android.</p>
  <p>Students with a school email can get the Plus plan free. You can build a study dashboard with a subjects database, an assignments board and linked lecture notes. <strong>Weak spots:</strong> nothing is ready-made. There is no built-in class timetable, exam countdown or grade calculator, and set-up can take a weekend.</p>

  <h3 id="printable">6. Printable study planner templates: best free option with no screen</h3>
  <p>Our <a href="/templates/">free printable templates</a> include a weekly planner, a daily planner, an exam revision tracker, an assignment tracker and a semester calendar. Writing the week by hand forces you to decide when and where you will study, which is the kind of plan research links to following through (Gollwitzer &amp; Sheeran, 2006). <strong>Weak spots:</strong> no reminders, no repeating classes, and it is gone if you lose it.</p>

  <h3 id="web-tools">7. Free browser tools: best for a one-off plan in minutes</h3>
  <p>If you need a plan today and do not want an account, use the <a href="/tools/study-plan-generator/">study plan generator</a> (subjects and an exam date in, a day-by-day plan out), the <a href="/tools/timetable-maker/">timetable maker</a> (print it or add it to your calendar) or the <a href="/tools/exam-countdown/">exam countdown</a>. They run entirely in your browser; nothing you type is sent to a server.</p>

  <h2 id="cost">What “free” can cost you, and how to check</h2>
  <p>Free planners make money somehow. That is fine, as long as you know how before you put a semester of deadlines into one.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Hidden cost</th><th scope="col">How to check in 2 minutes</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Limits that bite later</strong> (one semester, a few grades, a few AI uses)</td><td>Read the free vs paid table on the app’s pricing page. Ask: will I hit this limit every week?</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Ads</strong></td><td>On Google Play, look for “Contains ads” under the app name.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Your data</strong></td><td>On Google Play, open “Data safety” to see what the app collects and shares. Then skim the privacy policy for “sell” and “third parties”.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Lock-in</strong></td><td>Check whether you can export your data or keep it if you stop paying. (On ClassTrack, your data stays on the free plan if you cancel Pro.)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Trial, not free</strong></td><td>If the store says “free trial” or the app asks for a card on day one, it is a paid app with a trial.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="setup">How to set up a free study planner in 30 minutes</h2>
  <p>These steps work in any planner on this page. Do them in order; each one feeds the next.</p>
  {steps.map((s, i) => (
    <>
      <h3 id={s.id}>Step {i + 1}: {s.name}</h3>
      <p>{s.text}</p>
    </>
  ))}
  <p>For a fuller walk-through, see <a href="/guides/how-to-use-a-study-planner-app/">how to use a study planner app</a>. To split planning between a laptop and a phone, see the <a href="/guides/online-study-planner-laptop-and-phone/">laptop and phone routine</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="work">Make it work: what the research says</h2>
  <p>A free planner does as much good as a paid one if you use it to do the things that work.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Plan the week, not just the day.</strong> Among 90 college students, short-range planning predicted cumulative GPA, and did so better than SAT scores (Britton &amp; Tesser, 1991).</li>
    <li><strong>Give every study block a time, place and task.</strong> Across 94 studies, deciding exactly when and where you will act had a medium-to-large effect on reaching goals (Gollwitzer &amp; Sheeran, 2006).</li>
    <li><strong>Leave slack.</strong> Students expected to finish their thesis in about 34 days; it took about 56 on average (Buehler, Griffin &amp; Ross, 1994). Keep about 20% of the week unbooked.</li>
    <li><strong>Space your revision and test yourself.</strong> Of ten popular techniques, only spaced practice and practice testing were rated high utility (Dunlosky et al., 2013). Use the planner to put the same subject on several days, and end blocks with a short self-test.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>More detail: <a href="/guides/evidence-based-study-techniques/">active recall and spaced repetition: what the research says</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="choose">Which free study planner should you choose?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Android phone, or a laptop and a phone:</strong> ClassTrack (Android app plus web app on one account).</li>
    <li><strong>iPhone and you want a native app with reminders:</strong> MyStudyLife.</li>
    <li><strong>You mainly track grades and dislike subscriptions:</strong> Power Planner.</li>
    <li><strong>Your school runs on Google Classroom:</strong> Google Calendar, plus a planner for homework and exams.</li>
    <li><strong>You love designing systems:</strong> Notion.</li>
    <li><strong>Screens distract you:</strong> a printable weekly planner, with exam dates in your phone calendar for alerts.</li>
    <li><strong>You need a plan tonight:</strong> the free <a href="/tools/study-plan-generator/">study plan generator</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Want paid options too? See our full comparison of the <a href="/guides/best-study-planner-apps/">9 best study planner apps</a>.</p>

